Below is the detailed goal model decomposition for the described multi‐robot resource collection and delivery mission. In this model, we break down the overall mission into phases (collection and delivery), further decompose each phase into sub‐goals and tasks, and include fallback alternatives for low battery conditions. We also note that when multiple storages are involved, the individual “collect resource” operations can be executed in parallel across different robots to reduce overall mission time.

─────────────────────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Define the Top‐Level Goal  
─────────────────────────────────────────────

• G1: Complete Resource Collection and Delivery Mission  
  – Text: Ensure that the required resources are collected from the appropriate storages and then delivered to the requesting agent at the specified location.  
  – Type: Achieve (because the mission’s target state is “all resources delivered to the specified location”).  
  – Target Condition: All required resources have been delivered.  
  – Runtime: Sequential (“;”) because the collection phase must be completed before the delivery phase starts.  
  – Relation: AND (both collection and delivery are mandatory).  
  – Justification: The mission is composed of two essential sequential phases: first, resource collection; then, delivery.  

─────────────────────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Decompose the Collection Phase  
─────────────────────────────────────────────

Within the collection phase, we have two alternatives: the normal path and a fallback if the battery runs low (<10%). We represent the fallback using the Fallback operator.

• G1.1: Collection Phase  
  – Text: Execute the resource collection from storages.  
  – Type: Perform (because it involves a series of actions).  
  – Runtime: FALLBACK((G1.1.1;G1.1.2), G1.1.3)  
  – Relation: Fallback (normal collection vs. low battery handling).  
  – Justification: Under normal conditions, the robot computes the optimal storage order and then collects the resource. However, if the battery falls below 10% during collection, the fallback is triggered.

• G1.1.1: Determine Optimal Storage Order  
  – Text: Calculate the order of storages to visit based on the estimation of waiting times and path lengths.  
  – Type: Perform  
  – Runtime: Sequential (“;”)  
  – Relation: AND (all computational steps must be completed)  
  – Justification: The order is computed by sequentially performing: (a) computing waiting times, (b) computing path lengths, and (c) aggregating the results.  

• G1.1.2: Collect Resource from Storage  
  – Text: Navigate to the designated storage, send the resource request with precise specifications, and wait until the storage retrieves the resource.  
  – Type: Perform  
  – Runtime: Sequential (“;”)  
  – Relation: AND (each step is necessary; note that if multiple storages are involved, multiple instances of this goal can run in parallel across robots)  
  – Justification: The operations of navigation, communication, and waiting are performed sequentially for each storage; however, when several items are needed from different storages, different robots can execute these tasks concurrently.  

• G1.1.3: Handle Low Battery in Collection  
  – Text: When the battery level falls below 10% during collection, return to the recharging station and assign the mission to another robot.  
  – Type: Perform  
  – Runtime: Sequential (“;”)  
  – Relation: AND  
  – Justification: This fallback action is triggered by low battery and ensures continuity of the mission by recharging and reassigning the task.

─────────────────────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Decompose the Delivery Phase  
─────────────────────────────────────────────

In the delivery phase, similar sequential operations are required. Additionally, a fallback is provided if the battery runs low (<30% during delivery).

• G1.2: Delivery Phase  
  – Text: Deliver the collected resources to the specified location.  
  – Type: Perform  
  – Runtime: Sequential (“;”)  
  – Relation: AND  
  – Justification: The delivery process is composed of sequential actions that must be completed to ensure all resources are delivered.

• G1.2.1: Deliver Resources  
  – Text: For each delivery run, navigate to the destination and deliver the resource.  
  – Type: Perform  
  – Runtime: Sequential (“;”)  
  – Relation: AND  
  – Justification: Each delivery run requires sequential execution of navigation and actual resource delivery.

• G1.2.2: Handle Low Battery in Delivery  
  – Text: When the battery falls below 30% during delivery, return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining task. If that fails, trigger an alert and send a report to the sector manager.  
  – Type: Perform  
  – Runtime: FALLBACK((AT11;AT12), AT13)  
  – Relation: Fallback (primary action vs. alert)  
  – Justification: The primary fallback action is to return the resource and reassign the task; if that does not succeed, an alert must be triggered as a further fallback.

─────────────────────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Define the Task Nodes  
─────────────────────────────────────────────

Below are the concrete tasks (leaf nodes) corresponding to each goal’s sub-goals.

• G1.1.1 Tasks:  
  – AT1: Compute Waiting Times  
   • Text: Calculate the waiting times for each storage location.  
   • Location: onboard computer  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Each robot performs this computation internally.  
  – AT2: Compute Path Lengths  
   • Text: Calculate the path lengths from the robot’s current position to each storage.  
   • Location: onboard computer  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Necessary for determining the most efficient route.  
  – AT3: Aggregate Order  
   • Text: Combine waiting times and path lengths to determine the optimal order of storage visits.  
   • Location: onboard computer  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: This final aggregation step is done onboard before the robot proceeds.

• G1.1.2 Tasks:  
  – AT4: Navigate to Storage  
   • Text: Move the robot to the designated storage location.  
   • Location: storage  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Physical navigation is required to reach the storage.  
  – AT5: Send Resource Request  
   • Text: Send a message with the precise specification of the requested resource to the storage.  
   • Location: storage  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Communication must occur at the storage for resource retrieval.  
  – AT6: Wait for Retrieval  
   • Text: Wait until the storage retrieves and provides the resource.  
   • Location: storage  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: The robot must remain at the storage until the resource is available.

• G1.1.3 Tasks:  
  – AT7: Return to Recharging Station  
   • Text: Navigate the robot back to the recharging station when the battery is low.  
   • Location: recharging station  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Ensures the robot’s battery is recharged to continue the mission.  
  – AT8: Reassign Mission to Another Robot  
   • Text: Transfer the current mission to another robot for continuity.  
   • Location: mission control  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Maintains mission progress when the primary robot cannot continue due to low battery.

• G1.2.1 Tasks:  
  – AT9: Navigate to Destination  
   • Text: Move the robot to the specified delivery location.  
   • Location: destination  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Required to complete the delivery run.  
  – AT10: Deliver Resource  
   • Text: Place the resource at the specified destination.  
   • Location: destination  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Actual physical delivery of the resource.

• G1.2.2 Tasks:  
  – AT11: Return Resource to Checkpoint  
   • Text: Navigate the robot to a checkpoint to return the resource when battery is low.  
   • Location: checkpoint  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Ensures that the resource is not lost during low battery conditions.  
  – AT12: Reassign Remaining Task  
   • Text: Transfer the remaining delivery task to another robot.  
   • Location: mission control  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Continues the delivery process if the current robot cannot complete it.  
  – AT13: Trigger Alert and Send Report  
   • Text: Send an alert and report the failure to return the resource to the sector manager.  
   • Location: mission control  
   • Number of Robots: 1  
   • Justification: Acts as a final safety measure if the fallback in delivery fails.

─────────────────────────────────────────────  
Step 5. Summary of Logical Relationships  
─────────────────────────────────────────────

• G1 → G1.1 and G1.2 [AND]  
  – Justification: Both the collection and delivery phases are essential and must be executed sequentially to complete the mission.

• G1.1 → G1.1.1 and G1.1.2 and G1.1.3 [Fallback]  
  – Justification: The normal collection path (order determination and resource collection) is primary; if the battery drops below 10%, the fallback (low battery handling) is executed.

• G1.1.1 → AT1, AT2, AT3 [AND]  
  – Justification: All computation steps (waiting times, path lengths, aggregation) must be completed sequentially to determine the optimal storage order.

• G1.1.2 → AT4, AT5, AT6 [AND]  
  – Justification: For each storage, the robot must sequentially navigate, request, and wait for retrieval. (Note: When multiple storages are involved, these instances can run in parallel across robots.)

• G1.1.3 → AT7, AT8 [AND]  
  – Justification: When the battery is low during collection, the robot must return to recharging and reassign the mission.

• G1.2 → G1.2.1 and G1.2.2 [AND]  
  – Justification: The delivery phase includes both the normal delivery process and a fallback for low battery conditions.

• G1.2.1 → AT9, AT10 [AND]  
  – Justification: Each delivery run requires sequential navigation to the destination and the actual delivery of the resource.

• G1.2.2 → AT11, AT12, AT13 [Fallback]  
  – Justification: In delivery, if the battery is low, the primary fallback is to return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the task; if that fails, an alert is triggered.

